{
	"nodes":[
		{"id":"6116fdc086470f83","type":"file","file":"thesis/ref_papers/article_haloscope.pdf","x":257,"y":-420,"width":620,"height":483},
		{"id":"4bfea72f3c6449f2","type":"text","text":"### Embeddings Normalization\nFactorization may be performed on any layer of LLM representations\n1. Extract embeddings from LLM\n2. Perform normalization and SVD decomposition\n3. Hallucinated samples lay further from origin![[Pasted image 20260915192300.png]]","x":1080,"y":-920,"width":360,"height":500,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"4e094d827e5c1ade","type":"text","text":"# HaloScope\nLearning fraemwork that leverages unlabeled LLM generation in the wild for hallucination detection.$$P_{unlab} = (1 - \\pi)P_{true} + \\pi P_{hal}$$, where $\\pi$ - mixing ratio\n```\nThe intuition is that hallucinated samples can be captured\nby a small subspace, allowing them to be distinguished from the truthful samples\n```\n","x":717,"y":-900,"width":320,"height":460,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"0f61e29831c76ac3","type":"text","text":"### Membership Estimation\n$$\\zeta_i = \\frac{1}{k}\\sum_{j=1}^k \\sigma_j \\langle f_i, v_j \\rangle^2$$,where:\n* $v_j$ column from V\n* $\\sigma_j$ corresponding singular value\n* $k$ # of spanning directions in subspace","x":919,"y":-346,"width":341,"height":306,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"341531a4525df150","type":"text","text":"Generating a reliable truth dataset for\nhallucination detection requires **human annotator**","x":400,"y":-840,"width":267,"height":140,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"8bb2622456acfba2","type":"text","text":"Hallucination detection performance initially increases from the **top to middle layers** (e.g., 8-14th layers), followed by a subsequent decline.","x":919,"y":40,"width":368,"height":115,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"6c5fa8d36d00939d","type":"text","text":"This trend suggests a gradual capture of **contextual information by LLMs in the first few layers,** followed by a tendency towards **overconfidence in the final layers** due to the autoregressive training objective aimed at vocabulary mapping.","x":1320,"y":-178,"width":373,"height":182,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"675e475216854b4e","type":"text","text":" LLaMA model tends to encode the hallucination information mostly in the output of the transformer block while the most effective location for OPT models is the output of the feedforward layer,","x":1329,"y":180,"width":364,"height":157,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"311b5bd5e75479fa","type":"text","text":"Membership score later passed to classification model, that aims to distingluish hallucinated and true samples","x":1329,"y":-376,"width":351,"height":116,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"db899ccb768a66c7","type":"file","file":"thesis/ref_papers/article_hidden_states.pdf","x":317,"y":713,"width":713,"height":611},
		{"id":"5fd1fb6b32d653a4","type":"text","text":"For distingluishing hallucinated sample from true one, authors passes 2 questions (**S1**) with correct (**S3**) and incorrect (**S2**) answer. Since **last token accumaulates context of whole query**, it (more precisely hidden states) could be iused for hallucination detection","x":317,"y":432,"width":500,"height":180,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"e8836db8bbde2202","type":"file","file":"Pasted image 20260925075148.png","x":297,"y":180,"width":754,"height":169,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"514228920c91297b","type":"text","text":"Takes 2 states from last (32nd) transformer layer of Llama 2-7B. First one corresponds to last token in question **S1**, second one to last answer token (**S2 or S3**)","x":1160,"y":760,"width":300,"height":160,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"78eb5f9acf5352e9","type":"text","text":"**Correct answer** influences final state more that hallucinated one","x":1519,"y":617,"width":281,"height":96,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"402acaf8782570bc","type":"text","text":"We can derive **\"True\" and \"Hallucinated\" principal components** by finding difference of corresponding states.$$v_{hall} = s_1-s_2$$$$v_corr = s_1 - s_3$$","x":1580,"y":740,"width":311,"height":200,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"434b7329d245670d","type":"text","text":"Large and fine-tuned models tend to overconfidence, overlooking hallucination generation","x":1170,"y":1256,"width":302,"height":99,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"225162c94c21633e","type":"text","text":"Providing relevant knoweladge can increase model's awareness","x":1539,"y":1324,"width":250,"height":85,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"2b8e6c6e563a706b","type":"text","text":"Models demenstrates high awareness right after hallucinated output","x":1531,"y":1175,"width":269,"height":99,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"6697989564cfd9ae","type":"text","text":"Representations from middle layers (10-20 for Llama 2 models) provide more insights for hallucination detection","x":1170,"y":1019,"width":311,"height":131,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"cc4987646cdf5974","type":"text","text":"**TruthulQA** and **HaluEval**","x":860,"y":1520,"width":240,"height":60,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"549d26ca007f5c46","type":"text","text":"* Small models\n* Outdated models\n* Only short context window explored\n* All hallucinations are the same","x":176,"y":1493,"width":324,"height":167,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"b82feafef3d7eb80","type":"text","text":"* SVD used for decomposition lead to huge information loss","x":80,"y":-560,"width":324,"height":93,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"782cc0f996747421","type":"text","text":"* CoQA\n* TruthulQA\n* TriviaQA\n* TYDIQA-GP\n","x":-69,"y":-413,"width":229,"height":133,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"5d07bb9a1526a672","type":"text","text":"Comparing **S2** and **S3** we can get insights how hallucinations differ ftom true answers, and see its influence on future generations","x":880,"y":504,"width":369,"height":113,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"bab9df640d1752a9","type":"file","file":"thesis/ref_papers/article_internal.pdf","x":-760,"y":-22,"width":629,"height":544},
		{"id":"42c2405d4c57765f","type":"text","text":"Hidden state based detection offers the advantage of being reference-free, eliminating the need for external sources","x":-445,"y":1650,"width":302,"height":121,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"3c2c66d6a438813a","type":"text","text":" ICR score reflects an intrinsic property of the model rather than dataset-specific variations","x":-151,"y":1511,"width":268,"height":118,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"070c69efad37189d","type":"text","text":"* False positives for common tokens (punctuation, articles etc)\n* Open source models only\n* Only tested for QA tasks\n* Requires labels\n* Model specific","x":-85,"y":1238,"width":309,"height":193,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"06e240169738d2d5","type":"text","text":"* HaluEval\n* SQuAD\n* HotpotQA\n* TriviaQA","x":-63,"y":1045,"width":180,"height":141,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"9acf7eafce58d9fd","type":"text","text":"Hidden state update have form of$$x_i^l = x_i^{l-1} + a_i^l + m_i^l$$$$\\Delta x_i^l = x_i^l - x_i^{l-1} = a_i^l + m_i^l$$where\n* $l$ - layer num\n* $i$ - token\n* $a$ - attention factor\n* $m$ - FFN factor","x":-1223,"y":1351,"width":280,"height":360,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"9c4524891e53e47a","type":"text","text":"Authors explore hidden states ubpades by looking on attention and FFN contribution. They assume that shange in representation may help to detect hallucinated samples","x":-844,"y":1711,"width":316,"height":160,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"d3b3c9aa827a8b4a","type":"file","file":"thesis/ref_papers/article_hidden_state_dynamic.pdf","x":-823,"y":984,"width":631,"height":507},
		{"id":"a9fd444d55b50336","type":"text","text":"Middle layers (15-28 for Qwen) have the most critical role in hallucination detection","x":-809,"y":1564,"width":281,"height":107,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"5281d48bd5611e90","type":"text","text":"To compute ICR score:\n* Average attention scores smong all head to get 1 vector\n* Determine direction of hidden state update$$p_{ij}^l = \\frac{(\\Delta x_i^l)^Tx_j^l}{||x_j^l||}$$\n* Compare 2 distributions using Jensen-Shannon Divergence$$ICR_i^l=JSD(Proj_i^l,Attn_i^l)$$","x":-1302,"y":905,"width":317,"height":360,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"868617e8922c4c44","type":"text","text":"* Low ICR means that attention contributes more to update\n* High ICR means that FFN playing stronger role","x":-1685,"y":1116,"width":285,"height":175,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"bece07e152d21cde","type":"text","text":"The truth or falsehood of a statement should be represented by, and therefore extractable from, the LLM’s internal state","x":-421,"y":612,"width":290,"height":135,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"25702a50d1ce31fe","type":"text","text":"Authors check if LLM “believes” are true and those that are false using the hidden states to ","x":-588,"y":800,"width":285,"height":105,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"742e4ee0653ddbed","type":"text","text":"* Custom dataset designed for cross topic generation","x":-1110,"y":492,"width":250,"height":88,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"a3d86a2c0e006156","type":"text","text":"Middle layers contains stronger information about possible hallucinations","x":-780,"y":631,"width":273,"height":97,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"25be3a93e64f0e5f","type":"text","text":"Sentence probabilities pro- vide only a weak signal of the truth/falsehood of the sentence","x":-926,"y":757,"width":292,"height":96,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"5f266e628b333a17","type":"text","text":"SPALMA classifier uses models activations, directly passing them  to FFN. Activations are taken from 32, 28, 24, 20 and 16 layers","x":-1180,"y":288,"width":294,"height":122,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"5869ead5646a8ea7","type":"text","text":"* Binary Truth/false classification, no score\n* Works with isolated statements, may perform poorly for large context\n* Model specific","x":-1189,"y":13,"width":263,"height":187,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"10c3ad734dd3b2e1","type":"file","file":"thesis/ref_papers/article_deep_internal_representations.pdf","x":-707,"y":-640,"width":523,"height":496},
		{"id":"60456cffb6a6d034","type":"text","text":"Internal representations of LLMs encompass their awareness of whether the responses they generate are hallucinated or factual","x":-445,"y":-860,"width":305,"height":120,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"0c0205fb2703e358","type":"text","text":"Focus on the internal representation at the initial and final generation steps","x":-778,"y":-861,"width":258,"height":91,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"2147a8367ec84ec6","type":"text","text":"After LLMs process the query but before generating a response, they only encode the query without encoding any hal- lucination","x":-649,"y":-1020,"width":322,"height":127,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"dd30f54ab6b6b4bc","type":"text","text":"Authors use probing for choosing neurons, layers and representation, for further aggregation and passing to MLP for hallucination detection\nAdditionally, authors concatinate initial and final generation vectors","x":-1080,"y":-711,"width":265,"height":251,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"697c7966c37c4d25","type":"text","text":"* Custom **SOQHD** dataset based on TriviaQA and Natural Questions, filtered for data actuality\n* HaluEval","x":-1076,"y":-360,"width":261,"height":160,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"863253e7566b2860","type":"text","text":"Initial and final generation vectors are complimentary","x":-1132,"y":-866,"width":250,"height":66,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"1f4e2612c64f3401","type":"text","text":"* Simple models\n* QA based\n* Layer selection algorithm\n* No information why particular neurons encodes hallucanated information","x":-120,"y":-800,"width":260,"height":220,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"7cce3e0538d06fb6","type":"text","text":"**Hallutination** -  misleading or fabricated information","x":117,"y":-900,"width":250,"height":60,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"badb12b4b953ae3e","type":"text","text":"* **Is hallucination already visible in the internal state before the model produces the hallucinated token?**\n* **Could clustering algorithm be used for this?**\n* **How  to counter distribution drift with groving context?**","x":-2180,"y":805,"width":280,"height":305},
		{"id":"e4e25851560acd66","type":"file","file":"Pasted image 20260928230140.png","x":-2520,"y":868,"width":301,"height":400},
		{"id":"bc103088f24471c2","x":-1989,"y":-22,"width":649,"height":544,"type":"file","file":"thesis/ref_papers/article_hallucination_detection.pdf"},
		{"id":"452999dd770c3bd9","x":-1600,"y":612,"width":319,"height":129,"color":"4","type":"text","text":"Authors present line level hallucination detection, that may be more effictive thatn detection hallucinations in whole code sample"},
		{"id":"714fe86eebdea2fc","x":-2081,"y":630,"width":301,"height":98,"color":"6","type":"text","text":"* Custom dataset based on programming benchmarks Collu-Bench"},
		{"id":"6b9015737e35e465","x":-2325,"y":298,"width":244,"height":238,"color":"5","type":"text","text":"As features authors use:\n* hidden states\n* attention weights\n* token probs\n* token similarities\n* output ranks\n* lookback ration"},
		{"id":"a97844004850ad32","x":-2332,"y":107,"width":258,"height":134,"color":"5","type":"text","text":"FEatures passed to transformer classificator, for detecting hallucinated samples"},
		{"id":"a93e0d98e33461f7","x":-2180,"y":1144,"width":320,"height":316,"type":"text","text":"* In case of code generation, output/dataset samples must be normalized (compilation/linting?/variables and functions name normalization)\n* Check not lines but blocks of code (indents, brackets)\n* Comile code to intermediate state  ie diagram, logic etc (for checking algorithm logic)"},
		{"id":"81c9c132ec3716c7","x":-4140,"y":1317,"width":518,"height":243,"type":"text","text":"# Code\n```python\n1 def cube_Sum(n):\n2     sum = 0\n3     for i in range(1, n+1):\n4         sum += (2 * i) ** 3\n5     return sum\n```\n"},
		{"id":"fc5d70841cebe0c8","x":-3300,"y":1174,"width":360,"height":265,"type":"text","text":"# Plain Algorithm\n1. Set sum = 0\n2. for i = 0 in \\[....\\]\n\t1. set sum += (2 * i) ** 3\n\nAllows to eliminate situations like\n```python\nrange(1,n+1) != range(1, n + 1)\n```"},
		{"id":"eb9be5dfe4feb609","x":-3337,"y":1520,"width":416,"height":292,"type":"text","text":"# Vars/functions renaming\nInstead of using original names use smth like\n* sum = 0 -> var_1 = 0\n* def code_sum -> def func_1\n\nAllows to eliminate situattions like \n```python\nsum = 0 != nums = 0 # Same var dif names\n```"},
		{"id":"f84b8e0cf5226256","type":"text","text":"# Block level detection\nInstead of detect hallucination each line use block level detection with pushdown of required vars/funcs interfaces\n","x":-3300,"y":1860,"width":360,"height":160},
		{"id":"0e52ee2afe010b95","x":-2780,"y":1562,"width":260,"height":118,"type":"text","text":"# Detect hallucination in logic"},
		{"id":"6195946bedea62d8","x":-3540,"y":1317,"width":147,"height":53,"type":"text","text":"# Parcer"},
		{"id":"ddf938bef826c523","x":-3665,"y":1144,"width":250,"height":70,"type":"text","text":"Allows to drop code that not even compiles"},
		{"id":"ba646a52ebf675c5","x":-3518,"y":2101,"width":250,"height":60,"color":"1","type":"text","text":"Sometimes blocks can be overcomplicated"},
		{"id":"5b2f974a1bc6514b","x":-2160,"y":-956,"width":496,"height":494,"type":"file","file":"thesis/ref_papers/hallucination_heads.pdf"},
		{"id":"478c7f12fb9ca318","x":-2212,"y":-260,"width":263,"height":120,"color":"1","type":"text","text":"* DOmain specific tasks\n* ONly open source models\n* Model specific"},
		{"id":"1e32a761f385e605","x":-1529,"y":-568,"width":259,"height":108,"color":"4","type":"text","text":"Most informative features are derived from atten- tion maps of LLMs,"},
		{"id":"1f21a25c39185aaa","x":-1579,"y":-388,"width":279,"height":108,"color":"4","type":"text","text":"Attention weights reflect the condi- tional dependency between the generation steps"},
		{"id":"bef2bbef2a88bec2","x":-1564,"y":-1060,"width":264,"height":300,"color":"5","type":"text","text":"Authors present UQ head that consist of:\n* Feature reduction block\n* 2-layer classification model\n* GELU activation\n* Embedder layer\nHEad trained with binary cross entropy, with main LLMs weights freezed"},
		{"id":"709b57b90c98d1d3","type":"text","text":"Different neurons contribute differently to hallucanation detection","x":-1425,"y":-709,"width":250,"height":91,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"7e5f279fe89005b2","x":-1949,"y":-1120,"width":250,"height":60,"color":"6","type":"text","text":"* Custom dataset of english questions"},
		{"id":"51486d643c8728d5","x":-2240,"y":-393,"width":250,"height":67,"color":"1","type":"text","text":"* Domain specific \n* Only open models"},
		{"id":"d6f2c6fb14dca462","x":-3337,"y":-754,"width":303,"height":169,"color":"4","type":"text","text":"Response elicita-\ntion and groundtruth annotation strategy choices\ncan substantially affect probe quality"},
		{"id":"88501d2e12f21bed","x":-2941,"y":-744,"width":281,"height":149,"color":"4","type":"text","text":"More structured features are more robust un-\nder transfer, while raw hidden state and attention features are\nstrong in-domain"},
		{"id":"10cf6ed55791ca70","x":-3557,"y":-521,"width":285,"height":309,"color":"5","type":"text","text":"Each feature vector composer of 19 components:\n* Last embeddings\n* Mean embeddings\n* All embeddings\n* CoE-R and CoE-C\n* Cirvular Variance\n* SATMD\n* Loockback ratio\n* etc"},
		{"id":"afeb0fd0a76b5b49","x":-3157,"y":-429,"width":560,"height":529,"type":"file","file":"thesis/ref_papers/article_probes.pdf"},
		{"id":"520cffb3b34f045e","x":-3619,"y":-138,"width":282,"height":111,"color":"5","type":"text","text":"Features used to train MLP probes with ReLU activations and 1D-CNN"},
		{"id":"82b276cf08aef29a","x":-3640,"y":54,"width":303,"height":87,"color":"4","type":"text","text":"Reasoning hurts performance, for probe-based UE, concise and direct\ngenerations are preferable"},
		{"id":"8cb460ce5ca5c646","x":-2803,"y":141,"width":286,"height":185,"color":"4","type":"text","text":"higher dimensional features such as last-token em-\nbeddings can perform better with fewer training\nexamples, with less gains beyond 128 instances"},
		{"id":"36ea89df2c8cdb85","x":-3405,"y":198,"width":328,"height":135,"color":"4","type":"text","text":" Fusing complementary signal\ntypes are more useful than simply adding more of\nthe same type of hidden state features"},
		{"id":"c536a50cd35aabde","x":-3200,"y":364,"width":367,"height":92,"color":"4","type":"text","text":"Once feature representation is sufficiently\ninformative, linear probes are often competitive and more stable"},
		{"id":"0dd1c133369491b1","x":-2493,"y":-59,"width":168,"height":113,"color":"6","type":"text","text":"* TriviaQA\n* SciQ\n* PopQA"},
		{"id":"0869bd606689f0c0","x":-2517,"y":-298,"width":250,"height":134,"color":"1","type":"text","text":"* Suits only for entity centric fact chekcing\n* Open models\n* Only detection"}
	],
